NNAAMMEE
       pbmtopgm - convert PBM image to PGM by averaging areas


SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ppbbmmttooppggmm _w_i_d_t_h _h_e_i_g_h_t [_p_b_m_f_i_l_e]


DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppbbmmttooppggmm  reads  a PBM image as input.  It outputs a PGM image in which
       each pixel's gray level is the average of  the  surrounding  black  and
       white  input  pixels.   The surrounding area is a rectangle of _w_i_d_t_h by
       _h_e_i_g_h_t pixels.

       In other words, this is a convolution.  ppbbmmttooppggmm is similar to  a  spe‐
       cial case of ppnnmmccoonnvvooll.

       You may need a ppnnmmssmmooootthh step after ppbbmmttooppggmm.

       ppbbmmttooppggmm has the effect of anti-aliasing bitmaps which contain distinct
       line features.

       ppbbmmttooppggmm works best with odd sample width and heights.

       You don't need ppbbmmttooppggmm just to use a PGM program on a PBM image.   Any
       PGM  program (assuming it uses the Netpbm libraries to read the PGM in‐
       put) takes PBM input as if it were PGM, with only the minimum and maxi‐
       mum  gray  levels.  So unless your convolution rectangle is bigger than
       one pixel, you're not gaining anything with a ppbbmmttooppggmm step.

       The opposite transformation (which would turn a  PGM  into  a  PBM)  is
       dithering.  See ppaammddiitthheerrbbww.


